# CLOUDFLARE ORIGIN CERTIFICATE, NOT LET'S ENCRYPT.
#
# Every site block below pins an explicit `tls` certificate, which also turns
# Caddy's automatic HTTPS OFF for that site — it will not attempt ACME at all.
#
# WHY. Let's Encrypt could not issue: its SECONDARY validation perspectives
# consistently reported "networking error looking up A/AAAA" for all three
# hostnames, while the delegation, both authoritative nameservers, DNSSEC
# (cache-busted), UDP, TCP and EDNS all verified clean from two networks and
# four anycast resolvers, and LE reached this box and got HTTP 200 on the
# challenge. The error type was `acme:error:dns`, never `rateLimited`, and LE
# reported no incident. The cause sits in perspectives we cannot observe, so
# the fix routes around ACME rather than continuing to diagnose it.
#
# The certificate is issued by Cloudflare's Origin CA, valid 15 years, and is
# trusted ONLY by Cloudflare. THAT MAKES THE ORANGE CLOUD MANDATORY: with the
# proxy off, a browser hits this origin directly and rejects the certificate.
# The grey-cloud rule the docs used to carry existed to keep the HTTP-01
# challenge reachable; it is inverted now, because there is no challenge.
#
# SSL/TLS mode must be FULL (STRICT) in Cloudflare. Full alone accepts any
# certificate, including a forged one; strict verifies this CA, which is what
# makes the Cloudflare-to-origin hop authenticated rather than merely encrypted.
#
# Renewal: none for 15 years. Re-issue from SSL/TLS -> Origin Server and replace
# both files in /opt/wagerwolf/certs.

# Caddy terminates TLS and proxies to the app container.
#
# BACKEND_DOMAIN is supplied by compose. Set it to a real hostname
# (api.wagerwolf.app) and Caddy provisions and renews a Let's Encrypt
# certificate on its own — there is no certbot step and no cron to forget.
# Set it to ":80" to serve plain HTTP before DNS points here.
{$BACKEND_DOMAIN} {
	tls /etc/caddy/certs/origin.pem /etc/caddy/certs/origin.key
	reverse_proxy app:5000

	encode gzip zstd

	header {
		# The API is consumed by fetch() from the Vercel frontend, never framed
		# or rendered as a document, so these cost nothing and close the
		# cheapest classes of finding in a scan.
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		X-Frame-Options DENY
		Referrer-Policy no-referrer
		-Server
	}

	# Access logs stay in the container's stdout, which means `docker compose
	# logs caddy`. There is no log shipping configured — see DEPLOYMENT.md.
	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}
}

# THE SITE ITSELF, on the same box and the same Caddy as the API.
#
# Same-origin is the reason this is worth doing at all: the browser talks to
# wagerwolf.app for pages and api.wagerwolf.app for data, both terminating here,
# so there is one TLS setup and one place to reason about headers.
#
# NO `X-Frame-Options: DENY` HERE, unlike the API block. The API answers JSON
# and should never be framed; the site is a site, and denying framing outright
# breaks link previews and anything that embeds a page legitimately. The rest of
# the hardening applies to both.
{$APP_DOMAIN} {
	tls /etc/caddy/certs/origin.pem /etc/caddy/certs/origin.key
	reverse_proxy web:3000
	encode gzip zstd
	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin
		-Server
	}
	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}
}

# `www` redirects rather than serving a second copy: two hostnames serving
# identical content splits crawlers and cookies for no benefit. 308 preserves
# the method, so a POST that lands here is not silently downgraded to GET.
#
# CANONICAL_URL IS A FULL URL, SCHEME INCLUDED, and that is deliberate. This
# line used to be `redir https://{$APP_DOMAIN}{uri}`, which hardcodes the scheme
# and quietly assumes APP_DOMAIN is a bare hostname. It is in production — but
# running the stack locally means giving Caddy `http://site.localhost` to switch
# auto-HTTPS off, and the redirect then emitted `https://http://site.localhost/`.
# A 308 to a malformed URL is the kind of thing that works everywhere you test
# it and breaks the one place you did not.
{$WWW_DOMAIN} {
	tls /etc/caddy/certs/origin.pem /etc/caddy/certs/origin.key
	redir {$CANONICAL_URL}{uri} 308
}
